---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import QuizQuestion from "../../components/QuizQuestion.astro";
import { apiBasicsQuestions } from "../../data/questions";
---

<Layout title="API Basics | Aaron Holmes">
    <main class="container">
        <a href="/learn">← Back to Learn</a>

        <h1>API Basics</h1>

        <p>
            APIs allow different software systems to communicate with each
            other. Understanding them is an important part of building and
            troubleshooting automations.
        </p>
        <section>
            <h2>What is an API?</h2>

            <p>
                API stands for Application Programming Interface. An API
                provides a structured way for one application to request
                information or actions from another application.
            </p>

            <p>
                For example, an automation platform might use a CRM's API to
                create a new contact without a person opening the CRM and
                entering the contact manually.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Request and response</h2>

            <p>
                Most API interactions can be thought of as a conversation
                between two systems:
            </p>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    Your application sends a <strong>request</strong> to an API.
                </li>
                <li>The API processes the request.</li>
                <li>The API sends back a <strong>response</strong>.</li>
            </ol>

            <p>
                For example, an automation could send a request asking a CRM to
                create a new contact. The CRM might respond with the newly
                created contact's ID and other information.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Endpoints</h2>

            <p>
                An endpoint is a specific location in an API where a request can
                be sent. Different endpoints usually perform different actions
                or work with different types of data.
            </p>

            <p>
                For example, a CRM API might have one endpoint for contacts and
                another for deals.
            </p>

            <pre><code>https://api.example-crm.com/contacts</code></pre>

            <p>
                Think of the API as the building and an endpoint as a specific
                door. Which door you use depends on what you want to do.
            </p>
        </section>
        <QuizQuestion question={apiBasicsQuestions[0]} />
    </main>
</Layout>
